    <node id="sym:repo2graph/answer.py::_BoundedLines">
      <data key="d0">sym:repo2graph/answer.py::_BoundedLines</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">_BoundedLines</data>
      <data key="d10">_BoundedLines</data>
      <data key="d11">class</data>
      <data key="d3">repo2graph/answer.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">309</data>
      <data key="d13">360</data>
      <data key="d14">class _BoundedLines:</data>
      <data key="d15">Newline framing over a response body, with a hard byte ceiling.

    Three unbounded reads close here. The body arrives in fixed blocks, so no
    single newline-less line can grow without limit; the running total stops the
    stream once `limit` bytes have been taken, so a trickle of well-formed small
    deltas cannot either; and `deadline` stops it once the whole transfer has
    run longer than a real answer ever does, which is the case neither of the
    other two catches -- a host sending one byte at a time, slowly, stays under
    the byte ceiling forever and resets the socket timeout </data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="6595.32" y="541.05" width="117.70" height="28.60" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">_BoundedLines</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
