    <node id="sym:tests/test_answerability.py::test_a_test_definition_is_not_name_boosted">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_answerability.py::test_a_test_definition_is_not_name_boosted</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_a_test_definition_is_not_name_boosted</data>
      <data key="d10">test_a_test_definition_is_not_name_boosted</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_answerability.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">241</data>
      <data key="d13">261</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_a_test_definition_is_not_name_boosted(test_path_index):</data>
      <data key="d15">A test names the behaviour in the question's own words, so it collects the
    boost more reliably than the implementation does.

    `test_middleware_chaining_runs_handlers_in_order` overlaps "how are
    middleware handlers chained together" on two terms and would take the full
    boost; `compose_middleware`, which is the answer, overlaps on one and takes
    half. Boosting by name and demoting by path then fight, and the boost wins
    (2.0 * TEST_SEED_PENALTY = 1.2 still beats an unboosted source chunk), which
    reintroduces exactly the defect `TEST_SEED_PENALTY` exists to remove.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d17">9</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="0.00" y="2224.85" width="151.25" height="32.50" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">test_a_test_de…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
