    <node id="sym:tests/test_answerability.py::test_a_cheap_container_is_name_boosted">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_answerability.py::test_a_cheap_container_is_name_boosted</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_a_cheap_container_is_name_boosted</data>
      <data key="d10">test_a_cheap_container_is_name_boosted</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_answerability.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">272</data>
      <data key="d13">298</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_a_cheap_container_is_name_boosted(tmp_path):</data>
      <data key="d15">The container rule is one rule: a container too cheap to be penalised is
    also eligible for the boost.

    Sparing a chunk the penalty and then denying it the boost is not protection
    -- it leaves it to lose its slot to every sibling that can collect 1.5x.
    That is what dropped `app/store.py` from the demo's "trace an order request
    from route to persistence", the one starter question whose answer spans four
    modules, while `CONTAINER_PENALTY_MIN_TOKENS` was doing its job.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d17">127</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
