      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">139</data>
      <data key="d13">173</data>
      <data key="d14">def _require_working_cpp(path: Path) -&gt; None:</data>
      <data key="d15">Skip unless this machine can actually run the fallback on `path`.

    `shutil.which("cpp") is not None` is not that condition and was the reason
    this test flaked: on macOS `/usr/bin/cpp` always exists (it shells out to
    the Xcode toolchain), and the two spawns `parse_source` makes -- the
    `cpp --version` probe on a 5s timeout, then `cpp -w -P -undef` on a 10s one
    -- can each fail or time out on a loaded runner. Both failures are
    swallowed into `used_cpp=False`, so the assertion below reported "cpp citation accuracy
    regressed" for an environment that simply could not prep</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="2595.28" y="2746.45" width="151.25" height="32.50" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">_require_worki…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
