    <node id="sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_zizmor_ignore_pins_still_point_at_what_they_suppress">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_zizmor_ignore_pins_still_point_at_what_they_suppress</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_zizmor_ignore_pins_still_point_at_what_they_suppress</data>
      <data key="d10">test_zizmor_ignore_pins_still_point_at_what_they_suppress</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_prod_igy.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">1191</data>
      <data key="d13">1242</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_zizmor_ignore_pins_still_point_at_what_they_suppress():</data>
      <data key="d15">`.github/zizmor.yml` suppresses findings by `file:line`.

    Its own header says a shifted line number un-ignores the finding rather than
    silently keeping it suppressed -- but the reverse is worse and just as quiet:
    an edit above a pinned line slides the pin onto unrelated YAML, where it
    suppresses whatever finding lands there next. Only zizmor itself can prove a
    pin still matches, and zizmor is not a dependency of the pytest environment,
    so this pins the *construct* each entry was written for.

    The window is **per pin and tight**, which is the whole detector. An earli</data>
