    <node id="sym:tests/test_query_bounds.py::TestFitLines.test_fit_lines_is_linear_not_quadratic">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_query_bounds.py::TestFitLines.test_fit_lines_is_linear_not_quadratic</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_fit_lines_is_linear_not_quadratic</data>
      <data key="d10">TestFitLines.test_fit_lines_is_linear_not_quadratic</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_query_bounds.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">243</data>
      <data key="d13">276</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_fit_lines_is_linear_not_quadratic(self):</data>
      <data key="d15">Detector for fit_lines linearity. The old implementation called `measure` once
        per line either way -- the quadratic cost was in the *string building*,
        which no call counter can see -- so this has to be a wall-clock test.

        Comparing one input size against a larger one does not work here: the
        ceiling would be anchored on a small-input timing that is itself
        quadratic, and the two grow together. Measured, a 10x spread separates
        the regression from the allowance by only ~2x, which is how a timing
        test becomes a flake on a shared runner.

     </data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="3516.75" y="4085.85" width="145.20" height="31.20" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">TestFitLines.t…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
